        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Penlee Point, Rame; Plymouth Sound; Cornwall; Doom Bar; River Camel; Celtic Sea\n- Topics: Cornish place names; coastal geography\n\nFacts:\n- Cornwall is a ceremonial county in the United Kingdom.\n- Penlee Point, Rame is a coastal headland southeast of the village of Rame in southeast Cornwall, UK.\n- The Cornish name of Penlee Point is Penn Legh, meaning \"stone-slab headland.\"\n- Penlee Point lies at the entrance to Plymouth Sound.\n- Doom Bar was previously known as Dunbar sands, Dune-bar, and similar names.\n- Doom Bar is a sandbar at the mouth of the River Camel estuary, where it meets the Celtic Sea on the north coast of Cornwall, England.",
